WEBVTT

00:00.633 --> 00:03.200
(upbeat music)

00:12.933 --> 00:14.100
- Me?

00:14.100 --> 00:18.433
I'm a child of
Filipino immigrants

00:18.433 --> 00:23.100
and I am the father of
young Filipino children.

00:23.100 --> 00:27.267
I've been thinkin' a lot about
what it means to be Filipino

00:27.267 --> 00:30.167
As a second generation
Filipino American.

00:30.167 --> 00:33.100
I think about things like
how does identity change

00:33.100 --> 00:36.600
over time from one
generation to the next.

00:36.600 --> 00:39.067
I think about, do
our ethnic identities

00:39.067 --> 00:41.133
get watered down over time?

00:41.133 --> 00:44.633
I think about things like,
who gets to be American

00:44.633 --> 00:46.333
and who's a perpetual foreigner?

00:46.333 --> 00:51.000
Most of all, I think
where exactly is home?

00:52.200 --> 00:54.067
Some people assign
first generation

00:54.067 --> 00:57.167
to the US born
children of immigrants.

00:57.167 --> 00:58.967
So then, what does
that make the parents?

00:58.967 --> 01:00.667
Zero generation?

01:00.667 --> 01:05.333
So, let's once and
for all declare that
the first generation

01:06.067 --> 01:07.567
are the actual parents,

01:07.567 --> 01:10.467
the people who were born and
raised in another country

01:10.467 --> 01:13.033
and we're the ones that
came and established

01:13.033 --> 01:16.433
the new branch of the family
tree in the new country

01:16.433 --> 01:19.300
and their kids, that's
the second generation,

01:20.500 --> 01:21.900
people like me.

01:21.900 --> 01:25.000
The first generation
arrives in the new country.

01:25.000 --> 01:26.767
They hang on to what's familiar,

01:26.767 --> 01:28.033
but at the same time,

01:28.033 --> 01:30.467
they're also adapting,
they're acculturating.

01:30.467 --> 01:33.000
They learn to speak
the official language

01:33.000 --> 01:34.700
or they're forced to.

01:34.700 --> 01:38.500
The second generation, the
American born children,

01:38.500 --> 01:40.567
gets caught between two worlds.

01:40.567 --> 01:43.367
You might feel pressure
to carry on traditions

01:43.367 --> 01:45.567
that their parents are
handing down to 'em.

01:45.567 --> 01:48.667
They might feel compelled
to abandon traditions,

01:48.667 --> 01:53.033
to say, no that's not who we
are in order to try to fit in.

01:53.033 --> 01:55.000
Kids at school might tease 'em

01:56.233 --> 01:59.500
or they'll get made
fun of for foods

01:59.500 --> 02:00.733
that they bring to school

02:00.733 --> 02:01.933
and they'll grow up
to see the same foods

02:01.933 --> 02:04.067
on some white chefs
restaurant menu.

02:04.967 --> 02:06.367
Do you blend in?

02:06.367 --> 02:08.400
Do you assimilate?

02:08.400 --> 02:10.467
Or do you uphold tradition?

02:10.467 --> 02:13.667
Most often we actually always
do a little bit of both

02:13.667 --> 02:15.933
and then I went and had kids.

02:15.933 --> 02:18.767
The third generation,
my children.

02:18.767 --> 02:21.833
Is the third generation
even Filipino anymore?

02:21.833 --> 02:26.633
I witnessed the phenomenon
of third generation children

02:26.633 --> 02:31.067
who end up actually riding
harder for their heritage

02:31.067 --> 02:33.700
than their second
generation parents did.

02:33.700 --> 02:36.100
This kinda, it's almost as if

02:36.100 --> 02:39.133
they're trying to
undo the assimilation.

02:39.133 --> 02:40.733
Sometimes it goes
back and forth.

02:40.733 --> 02:42.000
Sometimes it curves.

02:42.000 --> 02:43.367
It's constantly zigzagging.

02:45.733 --> 02:48.433
We're choosing who we are
while at the same time,

02:48.433 --> 02:52.367
a larger society seems
to be choosing for us

02:52.367 --> 02:53.600
and we're negotiating,

02:53.600 --> 02:56.200
we're constantly being
pushed and pulled.

02:56.200 --> 02:58.600
In a way, regardless
of geography,

02:58.600 --> 03:01.700
I think every generation
is a first generation.
